<div class="lang-en">
<section id="key-dates">
<h2>When is Chinese New Year 2027?</h2>
<p><strong>Chinese New Year falls on Saturday, February 6, 2027.</strong> The date begins the first month of the Chinese lunisolar calendar and the customary Spring Festival period. In the sexagenary calendar, the year is called <strong>Ding Wei (丁未)</strong>, the <strong>Fire Goat</strong> year. The animal 羊 (<em>yáng</em>) can be rendered Goat, Sheep, or Ram in English; all refer to this same Earthly Branch.</p>
<p>For the Chinese calendar's computation and national standards, our primary institutional reference is the <a href="https://pmo.cas.cn/xwdt2019/kpdt2019/202203/t20220309_6386774.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Purple Mountain Observatory (紫金山天文台), Chinese Academy of Sciences</a>, which compiles China's astronomical almanacs and led the drafting of <a href="https://std.samr.gov.cn/gb/search/gbDetailed?id=n4aXcLrEnvA%3D&amp;mode=p" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GB/T 33661-2017, Calculation and Promulgation of the Chinese Calendar</a>. For the specific dates in 2027, the <a href="https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/gts/time/calendar/pdf/files/2027e.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Hong Kong Observatory's 2027 conversion table</a> records the first lunar month and solar terms, and the <a href="https://www.si.edu/spotlight/lunar-year-goat" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Smithsonian Institution</a> confirms the date and zodiac animal. <strong>Li Chun (立春)</strong> falls on <strong>February 4, 2027</strong>. A standalone 2027 calendar table issued by Purple Mountain Observatory has not been located in its public almanac index, so we do not attribute these specific 2027 dates directly to an unverified publication.</p>
</section>
<section id="fire-goat">
<h2>What does Ding Wei (丁未) mean?</h2>
<p>The traditional sixty-year cycle combines ten <a href="/heavenly-stems-earthly-branches/">Heavenly Stems</a> with twelve Earthly Branches. <strong>Ding (丁)</strong> is the fourth stem, associated with Yin Fire; <strong>Wei (未)</strong> is the eighth branch, associated with 羊 (Goat/Sheep). This gives 2027 the common label <strong>Yin Fire Goat</strong>. The previous Ding Wei year was 1967; the combination returns every 60 years.</p>
<p>Traditional zodiac readings connect branches, five phases, and symbolic interpretations. These are cultural and interpretive traditions, not scientifically established predictions of an individual's personality or future. For the year within the longer cycle, see our <a href="/zodiac-year/2027/">2027 zodiac reference page</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="two-boundaries">
<h2>Chinese New Year versus Li Chun</h2>
<p>These are different calendar markers. <strong>Chinese New Year (正月初一)</strong> is the first day of the first lunar month and marks the festival. <strong>Li Chun (立春)</strong> is the Beginning of Spring solar term, determined by the Sun's position. Some <a href="/bazi/">Four Pillars (BaZi)</a> methods use Li Chun as the boundary for the year's pillar; popular zodiac descriptions often use Chinese New Year instead.</p>
<p>For birthdays close to February 4–6, 2027, state which convention is being used. Do not assume January 1 begins the new Chinese zodiac year, and do not treat the two Chinese calendar boundaries as interchangeable. Exact birth times and time zones can matter for a BaZi chart.</p>
</section>
<section id="historical-context">
<h2>Pre-Qing historical context</h2>
<p>The calculation of the lunisolar calendar and its solar terms long predates the Qing dynasty. Early Han works including the <em>Huainanzi</em> (淮南子), particularly the <em>Tianwenxun</em> (天文訓), discuss the seasonal solar-term system; the <em>Hou Hanshu</em> (後漢書, Book of the Later Han), in its calendar treatise, also preserves technical calendrical material. These historical texts document calendar concepts, not a modern prediction about the luck of people born in 2027.</p>
<p>Regional customs associated with Spring Festival have changed across centuries. We therefore distinguish calendar dates supported by modern astronomical calculations from the historical record of festival practices and later folklore.</p>
</section>
